<section id="s-intro" data-code="intro">
<div class="section-no">01 — The Problem</div>
<h2>Why Full Rollouts Hurt On-Policy Distillation</h2>
<p>
  Standard on-policy distillation (OPD) trains a student language model on rollouts generated by itself,
  receiving token-level feedback from a teacher model at each position. While this approach avoids the
  need for fixed demonstrations, it introduces two key inefficiencies in long-horizon reasoning tasks:
</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Computational cost:</strong> Generating full rollouts requires substantial decoding time, KV cache memory, and log-probability computation.</li>
  <li><strong>Noisy feedback:</strong> As the student policy diverges from the teacher during training, late rollout positions enter regions where teacher feedback becomes unreliable, corrupting gradients for early tokens through return-to-go accumulation.</li>
</ul>
<p>
  This problem is especially pronounced in mathematical reasoning, where multi-step solutions demand long rollouts and early mistakes can derail the entire trajectory.
</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">Trajectory Canvas</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">Drag to see the contrast</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <!-- We'll implement the canvas visualization here -->
    <canvas id="trajectory-canvas"></canvas>
    <div class="canvas-controls">
      <button id="btn-full">Full Rollout (OPD)</button>
      <button id="btn-truncated">Truncated Rollout (TOPD)</button>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
</section>

<section id="s-background" data-code="background">
<div class="section-no">02 — Background</div>
<h2>How On-Policy Distillation Works</h2>
<p>
  OPD aligns the student model with the teacher by computing the log-ratio of their probabilities at each generated token. Unlike supervised fine-tuning, which uses fixed demonstrations, OPD exploits the student’s own generation distribution.
</p>
<p>
  Two main variants exist:
</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Token-level OPD:</strong> Each token is aligned only with the teacher at the same position (horizon γ=0).</li>
  <li><strong>Sequence-level OPD:</strong> Each token receives supervision from the accumulated future log-ratios (horizon γ=1), making early tokens sensitive to unreliable late feedback.</li>
</p>
<p>
  The key concept is the <strong>rollout horizon</strong>: the number of future tokens whose feedback contributes to the current token’s update.
</p>

<div class="vp">
