<section id="s-method" data-code="method">
<div class="section-no">03 — Core Idea</div>
<h2>Controlling the Rollout Horizon</h2>
<p>
  The insight is that full rollouts are not necessary for effective OPD. Since token-level OPD does not require a final answer reward, even truncated rollout segments can provide meaningful supervision. By controlling the rollout horizon, we can reduce computation while focusing on reliable early prefixes.
</p>
<p>
  We introduce two strategies:
</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Progressive OPD (POPD):</strong> Start with a short rollout horizon and gradually expand it during training, allowing the student to first learn reliable prefixes before tackling longer reasoning.</li>
  <li><strong>Truncated OPD (TOPD):</strong> Permanently use a truncated rollout horizon, distilling only the prefix where teacher feedback is most reliable.</li>
</p>

